<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#porcelain">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Porcelain</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Porcelain is a fine, hard, translucent ceramic first developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Chinese porcelain was a major export from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> onwards, and Ming and Qing wares were in great demand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, drawing large flows of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> to China. It was also produced in Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and trade with China brought porcelain to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/porcelain.jpg" alt="Porcelain" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B1%9D%E7%AA%AF_%E9%9D%92%E7%93%B7%E8%86%BD%E7%93%B6.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
